  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLow pressure is located over the Upper Midwest with a cold front extending\nsouth through the Mississippi Valley. Radar imagery shows a swath\nof rain ahead of a deep upper level trough that will curl northeast\ntowards the Great Lakes Region today. Rain is forecast to reach\nthe I-75 corridor towards sunrise on the nose of a 45 knot low \nlevel jet and expand east today. The deep moisture axis shifts \neast with time with most areas receiving between a quarter and \nhalf inch of rain. Locally higher rain amounts are focused \ntowards Central Ohio. Temperatures will be warmer in the east \ntoday where most of the morning will be dry. Looking upstream a\nbroken S type radar signature in Indiana is starting to fall \napart as it moves northeast. Fortunately there is little to no \nsurface based instability so this trend is expected to continue\nwith no hazardous weather expected. We will see southerly \nwinds gusting to around 30 mph in Northwest Ohio as the gradient\ntightens and low level jet arrives. Once the rain arrives fills\nin the potential for stronger wind gusts will be reduced due to\nstable boundary layer conditions. Have included just a slight \nchance of thunderstorms in portions of Northwest and North \nCentral Ohio but so far not seeing any lightning upstream in \nIndiana. \n\nRain will tend to exit to the east by Thursday morning as the dry \nslot sets up overhead. Favorable mixing should help clear clouds out \nand mix into drier air aloft. High temperatures are forecast to be \nin the low 60s on Thursday with breezy southwest winds and clearing \nskies. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAn upper level trough will dive south out of Canada \non Thursday and phase with the upper low over the Great Lakes \nRegion. Another trough will race northeast from the Four Corners \nregion on Thursday night and bring a plume of deeper moisture back \ntowards the local area. Showers will tend to expand from west to \neast on Friday as a cold front wraps around the south side of the \nupper level low. Showers will blossom across the area, especially \nduring the afternoon. Another dry-slot does wrap into the system on \nFriday evening but wrap around moisture through 700mb is forecast to \nreturn and be focused across the east half of Lake Erie on Saturday. \nTemperatures on Friday will be in the 50s with the highest values in \nthe east ahead of the front. High temperatures on Saturday continue \nto trend downward as we end up on the back side of the upper level \nlow. Temperatures at 850mb will be as low as -3C on Saturday morning \nwhich should be sufficient for lake enhanced rain across the east \nhalf of the forecast area. Did not add thunder but can not rule out \na few isolated thunderstorms coming off Lake Erie. \n\nPrecipitation is generally expected to be done by Sunday with a \ngradual warming trend setting up for the early part of next week.",
